The discovery and development of new therapeutic drugs is costly and slow, and many candidate compounds fail during research and development [1]. A critical step in early-stage discovery is quantifying how strongly small molecules bind to disease-associated target proteins, measured as binding affinity, which reflects the strength of the compound-protein interaction [2, 3]. Accurate affinity prediction is therefore essential to distinguish strong, weak, and non-binding interactions, so that the most promising candidates can be prioritized for further investigation [3]. Measuring affinity experimentally across large numbers of drug-target pairs, however, demands substantial laboratory resources, time and cost [3]. This bottleneck has driven the rapid development of computational methods for predicting drug-target interactions, and more accurate, validated predictions could accelerate the development of novel therapeutics [4, 5].
This project therefore develops and evaluates machine-learning models that predict drug-target binding affinity from two inputs: the small-molecule structure, written as a Simplified Molecular-Input Line-Entry System (SMILES) string, and the protein amino-acid sequence, both obtained from the BindingDB database [6]. The task is regression: for a given drug-target pair, each model predicts a continuous binding affinity value (pKd). The intended use is scoring compounds that have not yet been assayed, so that laboratory measurement can be directed to the pairs most likely to bind, and our evaluation protocol is built to test exactly that setting.
